intro">A collection of web design and development news and thoughts from Co=
dePen's own Chris Coyier.</p>=0D=0A                          </mj-raw>=0D=
=0A                          <h3>Two Liners</h3>=0D=0A<p>The real <em>reaso=
n</em> that fancy modern CSS is exciting is because of what we can now <em>=
actually</em> do in CSS.</p>=0D=0A<p>I remember one of my favorite dudes Ha=
kim El Hattab made a thing called =E2=80=9CProgress Nav=E2=80=9D <em>ten ye=
ars ago</em>. The idea is a Table of Contents component that could tell whe=
re you are scrolled in the page and reflects that in the design of the ToC.=
 (<a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/0C7pxbblrxvNw7mtbtWAnA~~/AAB=
EfhA~/cQ5Ctqr1PGLHldMqSUzXqjiH9zCWAGhboCgmTRY2Bug0AvWPIwOg1ulPMpMpmYsCi03cV=
8vbM9GGURgbV0oABWwWPgP8IkF1jbW_3PURxtVq5SEkq-QMqzqTEBnExBFSZ5OFHAKDeTEGnKP-=
qRsF6dgfbKkkUmDt7RyriT-KxU0CejlU7au3cP3qbObMNbCnt3TCq64kMBOI3oG55IEOzQ~~">S=
ee GIF</a>)</p>=0D=0A<p>Perhaps you won=E2=80=99t be surprised that functio=
nality works via JavaScript. It=E2=80=99s not <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpo=
stmail2.com/f/a/eHWQm6skFy1ERG0fxgvZFQ~~/AABEfhA~/hp5APoHvB9zhranASH_E30l4D=
lf8jlnIgz0ShKHJeJB_QR0XIgILKbcK0bQ7mE1qqO3KXKnAvh9QeGAsFD5TwdkvO2iO18uEEy2T=
sJIG0-M6Wf3PXwMJMrGTfYWYB3W0_tmaYc3WCCvzpyjS7CrisTWt0WbM_IIpbx8V6KxOjDI~">h=
orridly huge or complex</a>, but, it does rely on totally-not-even-debounce=
d <code>window.addEventListener( 'scroll', ...</code> which is known to be =
a cause of janky performance.</p>=0D=0A<p>But it=E2=80=99s cool, right=3F!<=
/p>=0D=0A<p>This kind of thing has been come to be known as a =E2=80=9Cscro=
ll spy=E2=80=9D effect. I=E2=80=99m sure you can see where I=E2=80=99m goin=
g with this: <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/W723ix2jyuOO-_geU=
nuWiA~~/AABEfhA~/xXURB3wjwqImxbtypmtf7x1LR2mW2-W22UI-8IaDW1jQataFuQYXGs988l=
uLV17GMRkdTstMnDOwyhzH5rBCftQmzCq9SNpfPwKCjtVZILv-YtVFx_X3xh84Gm0qUgItFN5tr=
vpdRKmf71LqbBxe6w~~">CSS can do scroll spy now</a>. In Chrome anyway. Remem=
ber when all that <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/BQkhr43cgaXF=
Bbr3WLAWjg~~/AABEfhA~/HcMXY9jsuW4bpX2nkXw9ROKasDYArOnJv80MhCHjETze7K01zBziU=
Gf-h_ARyOnRM7HpojwLJX8_TVX8tbYMSisxnhC_ZXReUL28k1VxJNlaHvU9ZLZW4oT-D6jTLske=
V9gDPXq6ORv-jegbvvWkVk4WeDoxtyQlOs4VOG4mXTg~">CSS carousel</a> stuff was ha=
ppening=3F This is highly related. I remember people gently trying to say <=
em>this isn=E2=80=99t just CSS carousels, it=E2=80=99s interesting CSS prim=
itives that combined can make a carousel, but totally have other users too.=
</em> Well, here=E2=80=99s something.</p>=0D=0A<p><a href=3D"https://go.spa=
rkpostmail2.com/f/a/W723ix2jyuOO-_geUnuWiA~~/AABEfhA~/xXURB3wjwqImxbtypmtf7=
x1LR2mW2-W22UI-8IaDW1jQataFuQYXGs988luLV17GMRkdTstMnDOwyhzH5rBCftQmzCq9SNpf=
PwKCjtVZILv-YtVFx_X3xh84Gm0qUgItFN5trvpdRKmf71LqbBxe6w~~">Una=E2=80=99s blo=
g post is a live demo of it working.</a></p>=0D=0A<p>Here=E2=80=99s the mai=
n CSS that does it:</p>=0D=0A<pre><code class=3D"css">.parent {=0D=0A=09scr=
oll-target-group: auto;=0D=0A}=0D=0A=0D=0A:target-current {=0D=0A=09/* styl=
es for active anchor */=0D=0A}=0D=0A</code></pre>=0D=0A<p>Wow.</p>=0D=0A<hr=
 />=0D=0A<p>It=E2=80=99s also obviously ultra important that we <a href=3D"=
https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/82yHUXb-FNfaWNRTMx_9mA~~/AABEfhA~/kZh9-nC=
fU82hZaitZxiPfr4YMc3I8iugsClKLFMI8U5rt6DdJ0yJyfn6ZSDZ6ybZbPQd0gSZvYek3RYHSf=
eawEXycC1xnXvWFnaRBFpVjy8J_0iauXjamxKIgO7OCHcOsZ1CCUPtUQHdZnbFocoqc0hvXL87d=
c8w1w_-W8JSb5mMVDRw5UWDM9KA7Nsu57EV">can do Battlestar Galactica corners</a=
>, as Dave explains:</p>=0D=0A<blockquote><p>=E2=80=A6 the sci-fi film and =
television trope where all rectangles (doors, windows, furniture, user-inte=
rfaces) have one to three angular corners instead of all corners being the =
old, boring, un-futuristic 90=C2=BA rectangle corners. They are the cooler =
version of rectangles by all accounts.</p></blockquote>=0D=0A<p>Another two=
-liner =F0=9F=98=8D:</p>=0D=0A<pre><code class=3D"css">.sci-fi {=0D=0A=09bo=
rder-start-end-radius: 50px;=0D=0A=09corner-shape: bevel;=0D=0A}=0D=0A</cod=
e></pre>=0D=0A<img src=3D"https://production-codepen-email-assets.codepenas=
sets.com/cdn-cgi/image/width=3D600,quality=3D90,format=3Dauto/codepen-spark=
/galactica_paper.png" alt=3D"A Battlestar Galactica cut-corners effect crea=
ted with CSS corner-shape. " width=3D"450"/>=0D=0A<hr />=0D=0A<p>You know w=
hen an element moves when View Transitions are used=3F They say =E2=80=9Cth=
ey aren=E2=80=99t screenshots=E2=80=9D used during the moving/morphing=E2=
=80=A6 but=E2=80=A6 they kinda feel like they are screenshots. And so, some=
times it looks awkward. <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/gHI3ja=
ZMrQ4oyURIP5zBrQ~~/AABEfhA~/YOfo2qr4KrHYbkSMRt0-LH16raAXCVHELV61K7Lt20g0xLV=
-Jdu1r9GwBUe3FeyNEg4KJNRnHVMFNXxprmlBGHYMM1JGucEl7t5tYxDK7g_eK01kMhVYB3kT8W=
GpobWS576R2qdZ2MFt2WAcfwZbTd6dt43jwKNAQZtYQ80wO4U~">Bramus notes that an ex=
ample of this is when an element has a different border-radius</a>. Fixing =
this is more complicated than I=E2=80=99d like it to be and showcases how s=
ometimes these fancy features require a pretty deep dive once you get into =
the details. The good news is there is <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2=
.com/f/a/huZA1jYq-3SCvV-y3kAh4g~~/AABEfhA~/X7HCP_UQq8ZIMGAKkCeVcxZJhpm0kTP=
gMkgvvHDqLKZUtd-fsM2EBb4hsniDWRCG0e1oFE1cqLX_Jw7sOEhYSfZnitHLPo7woVJfQOHkRC=
STkDHJbsJp5_5bWdNUe0ie0iacV2CHatDSTi3PNbTiDQnxMsqwfBA-dRfWR4EN7QuUgr41Kid7u=
-8c90GI1hZN">a (slightly) simpler approach</a>, which requires thinking abo=
ut the individual parts you are actually animating. That part, thankfully, =
makes sense to me. Like giving the element itself and the guts of it differ=
ent names:</p>=0D=0A<pre><code class=3D"css">.card {=0D=0A=09view-transitio=
n-name: card;=0D=0A}=0D=0A=0D=0A.card &gt; .card-content {=0D=0A=09view-tra=
nsition-name: card-content;=0D=0A}=0D=0A</code></pre>=0D=0A<hr />=0D=0A<p>I=
